<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gwrtheyrnion</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny Welsh kingdom between the Wye and the Ithon whose rulers claimed descent from Vortigern, the British ruler blamed for inviting the Saxons.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gwrtheyrnion means 'the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Gwrtheyrn</a>', the Welsh form of Vortigern. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a>, written in 829 or 830, says that after Vortigern's fall his son Pascent was granted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-buellt">Buellt</a> and Gwrtheyrnion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrosius-aurelianus">Ambrosius</a>, and that its ruler in the author's day, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernmail">Fernmail</a>, descended from him. The territory lay around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhayader">Rhayader</a> in what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radnorshire">Radnorshire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Almost nothing is known of its government beyond this genealogy. It was a small upland territory of river valleys and hill pasture, often ruled together with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-buellt">Kingdom of Buellt</a>, and its dynasty used its link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a>, whatever its truth, to assert ancient legitimacy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Gwrtheyrnion was absorbed by more powerful Welsh kingdoms, probably in the 10th century, and later formed a cantref of the Middle March between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">Wye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">Severn</a>, held by Welsh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> lords such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mortimer">Mortimers</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 450 CE – 900 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path